      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777152943880,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Agent Orchestration Pattern\n\n- Single Agent Orchestration Strategy: Tool calling loops, ReAct, and plan-and-execute pattersn that help a single agent structure its thinging and execution\n    - Tool Calling loop\n        - Parse the document\n        - Call a Summarization tool\n        - Use a messaging API to send the result\n        Usecase: involve dynamic decisions or exploration. The looping structure allows the agent to act, observe outcomes, and iterate. Works for debugging, search or knowledge synthesis.\n    \n    - ReAct (reasoning + acting)\n        - Adds structure by alternating explicit reasoning steps with tool actions. The model is prompted to think step-by-step\n        Usecase: step-by-step transparency improves monitoring or user trust. Useful in regulated environments or ducation tolls where exposing the agents reasoning adds value.\n            \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n    \n    - Plan and Execute: First create full plan and executes each step.\n    Usecase: task can be cleanly broken into steps ahead of time. Ideal for workflows that demand sequential accuracy (booking travel or generating multi part document)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Multi Agent Collaboration Model: Manager-Worker setups, decentralized teams, and how agents can work together on shared goals.\n    - Manger-worker pattern\n        Usecase: Central planner to decompose the task and delegate it to specialized agents, Require clearly defined roles and a robus delegation strategy.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    - Decentralized handoff pattern: no central manager, agents operate as peers, each with its own responsibility. They pass control to one another based on the state of the task, or the type of input they encounter.\n        Usecase: operate independently, respond to environmental changes, not single agent has full context. (fits simmulations, real-time collaborative systems and distributed monitoring setups.)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Decision criteria for choosing patters: When should you go solo? When is coordination better? We'll walk through how to choose base don task complexirty and system needs.\n\n- Frameworks that helps: Langchain, CrewAI and AutoGen",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Agent Orchestration Pattern\n\n- Single Agent Orchestration Strategy: Tool calling loops, ReAct, and plan-and-execute pattersn that help a single agent structure its thinging and execution\n    - Tool Calling loop\n        - Parse the document\n        - Call a Summarization tool\n        - Use a messaging API to send the result\n        Usecase: involve dynamic decisions or exploration. The looping structure allows the agent to act, observe outcomes, and iterate. Works for debugging, search or knowledge synthesis.\n    \n    - ReAct (reasoning + acting)\n        - Adds structure by alternating explicit reasoning steps with tool actions. The model is prompted to think step-by-step\n        Usecase: step-by-step transparency improves monitoring or user trust. Useful in regulated environments or ducation tolls where exposing the agents reasoning adds value.\n            \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n    \n    - Plan and Execute: First create full plan and executes each step.\n    Usecase: task can be cleanly broken into steps ahead of time. Ideal for workflows that demand sequential accuracy (booking travel or generating multi part document)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Multi Agent Collaboration Model: Manager-Worker setups, decentralized teams, and how agents can work together on shared goals.\n    - Manger-worker pattern\n        Usecase: Central planner to decompose the task and delegate it to specialized agents, Require clearly defined roles and a robus delegation strategy.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    - Decentralized handoff pattern: no central manager, agents operate as peers, each with its own responsibility. They pass control to one another based on the state of the task, or the type of input they encounter.\n        Usecase: operate independently, respond to environmental changes, not single agent has full context. (fits simmulations, real-time collaborative systems and distributed monitoring setups.)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Decision criteria for choosing patters: When should you go solo? When is coordination better? We'll walk through how to choose base don task complexirty and system needs.\n\n- Frameworks that helps: Langchain, CrewAI and AutoGen",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
