      "updated": 1777157089209,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Guadrails\n\nsafety mechanisms that wrap around agent behaviour, operate alongside the model rather than within it. implemented as distinct modules that intercept data at key points\n\n- Input Guadrails: Applied before the LLM receives an input\n- Output Guadrails: Applied after LLM generates a response or plan\n- Tool-use Guadrails: Applied before a tool is executed\n\nContextual grounding checks:\n    drifting off topic or producing content unrelated to the task. agent generate information that is not supported by provided context, leading to hallucinations\n    Contextual grounding checks help ensure that an agents actions or putputs remain focused on the users intent and are factually supported by its knowledge sources.\n\n    - use retrieval based grounding (like RAG) to keep responses anchored to source content, explicitly preventing the agent from fabricating information.\n    - add prompt constraints that remind the model to stay on topic and only use provided context.\n    - apply post-response checks using classifiers or rule-based filters to assess topic alisngment and factual consistency against source material.\n\nSafety and moderation filters:\n risk that harmful, offensive or biased content might be genrated. Safety and moderation filters are designed to detect and block such content before it reaches the user or triggers downstream actions\n\n- use moderation APIS or classifiers trained to flag toxic, biased or unsafe language.\n- include system prompts that instruct the agent to avoid specific topics or behaviors.\n- define blocked content types and enforce them with post-generation checks.\n- rate limit sensitive capabilities to reduce the chance of misuse\nEg: hack payment system will stop this request at the input lvel.\n\n\nPII and data protection filters\n    Agent handle sensitive data, including user names, emails, credit card numbers\n\n    - user regular expressions or trained classifiers to detect PII in both inputs and outputs\n    - apply redaction or masking techniques before passing content to the model\n    - restrict which documents or data sources an agent can access using permission-based controls\n    - log and audit data access and response payloads for sensitive operations.\nEg: healtcase chatbot, patient records might be accessed through vector search. Guardrails ensure that only dat tied to the the current user or session is retrieved and any identifiers are masked in the response unless explicitly authorized.\nmeet regulatory standards like GDPR or HIPAA\n\n\nTool use safeguards\n    Ability to call APIs, modify databases, send messages, or control devices, risk of unintended or unsafe actions if the agent misuses a tool or calls it under the wrong conditions.\n\n    Allowlisting: Exlicitly define which tools an agent is allowed to use in a given context or role\n    Argument validation: Ensure that inputs to tools meet strict criteria before the tool is invoked\n    Preconditions: Use logic or model-generated reasoning steps to verify whether a tool should be used before execution.\n    Rate limits: Control how frequenctly tools can be used\n    Simulation before execution: Let the model generate a plan and simulate tool use without actually executing, then review or verify before action.\n\nRule based output Validation\nEven if an agent reasons correctly and uses tools appropriately the final response still needs to be checked.\nOutput validation ensures that what the agent says or sends out meets defined rules, whether those riles relate to tone, structure, content or safety\n\n    - Regex checks: Filter for forbidden content like profanity, private information or unsafe instructions.\n    - Format checks: Ensure the output matches a required structure (JSON schema or email template)\n    - Semantic checks: Use a separate model or post-processing step to verify that the response aligns with task requirements\n    - Blocklist and allowlists: Prevent the use of certain terms, require specific phrases to be included when necessary.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20.004120879120883,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Guadrails\n\nsafety mechanisms that wrap around agent behaviour, operate alongside the model rather than within it. implemented as distinct modules that intercept data at key points\n\n- Input Guadrails: Applied before the LLM receives an input\n- Output Guadrails: Applied after LLM generates a response or plan\n- Tool-use Guadrails: Applied before a tool is executed\n\nContextual grounding checks:\n    drifting off topic or producing content unrelated to the task. agent generate information that is not supported by provided context, leading to hallucinations\n    Contextual grounding checks help ensure that an agents actions or putputs remain focused on the users intent and are factually supported by its knowledge sources.\n\n    - use retrieval based grounding (like RAG) to keep responses anchored to source content, explicitly preventing the agent from fabricating information.\n    - add prompt constraints that remind the model to stay on topic and only use provided context.\n    - apply post-response checks using classifiers or rule-based filters to assess topic alisngment and factual consistency against source material.\n\nSafety and moderation filters:\n risk that harmful, offensive or biased content might be genrated. Safety and moderation filters are designed to detect and block such content before it reaches the user or triggers downstream actions\n\n- use moderation APIS or classifiers trained to flag toxic, biased or unsafe language.\n- include system prompts that instruct the agent to avoid specific topics or behaviors.\n- define blocked content types and enforce them with post-generation checks.\n- rate limit sensitive capabilities to reduce the chance of misuse\nEg: hack payment system will stop this request at the input lvel.\n\n\nPII and data protection filters\n    Agent handle sensitive data, including user names, emails, credit card numbers\n\n    - user regular expressions or trained classifiers to detect PII in both inputs and outputs\n    - apply redaction or masking techniques before passing content to the model\n    - restrict which documents or data sources an agent can access using permission-based controls\n    - log and audit data access and response payloads for sensitive operations.\nEg: healtcase chatbot, patient records might be accessed through vector search. Guardrails ensure that only dat tied to the the current user or session is retrieved and any identifiers are masked in the response unless explicitly authorized.\nmeet regulatory standards like GDPR or HIPAA\n\n\nTool use safeguards\n    Ability to call APIs, modify databases, send messages, or control devices, risk of unintended or unsafe actions if the agent misuses a tool or calls it under the wrong conditions.\n\n    Allowlisting: Exlicitly define which tools an agent is allowed to use in a given context or role\n    Argument validation: Ensure that inputs to tools meet strict criteria before the tool is invoked\n    Preconditions: Use logic or model-generated reasoning steps to verify whether a tool should be used before execution.\n    Rate limits: Control how frequenctly tools can be used\n    Simulation before execution: Let the model generate a plan and simulate tool use without actually executing, then review or verify before action.\n\nRule based output Validation\nEven if an agent reasons correctly and uses tools appropriately the final response still needs to be checked.\nOutput validation ensures that what the agent says or sends out meets defined rules, whether those riles relate to tone, structure, content or safety\n\n    - Regex checks: Filter for forbidden content like profanity, private information or unsafe instructions.\n    - Format checks: Ensure the output matches a required structure (JSON schema or email template)\n    - Semantic checks: Use a separate model or post-processing step to verify that the response aligns with task requirements\n    - Blocklist and allowlists: Prevent the use of certain terms, require specific phrases to be included when necessary.\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "6Lu-RFYQfkVw6OsnElA1t",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3007.8353794642876,
      "y": 1657.631138392859,
      "width": 1887.2386474609375,
      "height": 800,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aI",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 42248358,
      "version": 1362,
      "versionNonce": 1746925158,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777158834822,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Human oversight\n\n- Human-in-the-loop (HITL): A student driver with an instructor who must approve before making a turn\n    - Approval Gates: Before executing high-stakes or irreversible actions, the agent pauses and requires explicit human review and approval.\n    - Escalatoin triggers: agent's confidence in its decision is low, the input is ambigouse, or a policy is unclear, the agent defers control and hands off the task to a human expert.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Human-on-the-loop (HOTL): A driver using cruise control. The system is driving, but the human is watching and can take the wheel at any moment.\n    Intervention Interfaces: humans supervise agents via dashboards or dedicated interfaces, allowing them to step in, pause execution, override decisions, or edit plans\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Human-above-the-loop (HATL): Reviewing a car's trip data (GPS logs, fuel efficiency) after the journey is complete to see how it performed.\n     Audit trail and logging: Detailed logs of agent decisions, actions and system states allow human supervisors to retrospectively review what the agent did, why and what the outcomes were.\n         Important for accountability, diagonising failures, and identifying patterns for future improvements.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Human oversight\n\n- Human-in-the-loop (HITL): A student driver with an instructor who must approve before making a turn\n    - Approval Gates: Before executing high-stakes or irreversible actions, the agent pauses and requires explicit human review and approval.\n    - Escalatoin triggers: agent's confidence in its decision is low, the input is ambigouse, or a policy is unclear, the agent defers control and hands off the task to a human expert.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Human-on-the-loop (HOTL): A driver using cruise control. The system is driving, but the human is watching and can take the wheel at any moment.\n    Intervention Interfaces: humans supervise agents via dashboards or dedicated interfaces, allowing them to step in, pause execution, override decisions, or edit plans\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Human-above-the-loop (HATL): Reviewing a car's trip data (GPS logs, fuel efficiency) after the journey is complete to see how it performed.\n     Audit trail and logging: Detailed logs of agent decisions, actions and system states allow human supervisors to retrospectively review what the agent did, why and what the outcomes were.\n         Important for accountability, diagonising failures, and identifying patterns for future improvements.\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
